WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.680
<v Alex Morgan>Welcome back to The Long View. I'm joined by Skanda Amarnath, who reads the macroeconomy for a living. Skanda, good to have you.</v>

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.180
<v Skanda Amarnath>Thanks, Alex.</v>

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:17.100
<v Alex Morgan>Today's episode is sponsored by Bloomberg. Bloomberg Terminal is the data spine institutional desks run on. Try bloomberg.com today.</v>

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.160
<v Alex Morgan>You describe macro as risk management. Unpack that.</v>

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:32.460
<v Skanda Amarnath>Macro is risk management at scale. The job is mostly not being on the wrong side of a regime change — and the regime change you can name in advance is the one you can position for. The rest is just hoping.</v>

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:44.820
<v Skanda Amarnath>And the economy is a system, not a set of levers. Pull one and three others move. When we back-tested the calls, the misses were always someone treating a correlated system as if the parts were independent.</v>

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.360
<v Alex Morgan>So the same discipline Elena talks about in sustainability.</v>

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:58.800
<v Skanda Amarnath>Exactly the same. A macro book and a power grid are both systems of correlated risk. Systems thinking and risk management are one discipline — you just change the vocabulary.</v>

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.620
<v Alex Morgan>Thanks again to Morningstar for backing the show. Morningstar gives you independent fund ratings.</v>

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.120
<v Skanda Amarnath>Thanks, Alex.</v>

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.520
<v Alex Morgan>That's it for this Long View. Take care.</v>
